On Fri, 14 Jan 94 peb@procase.com (Paul Baclace) wrote:

	       >The cost of building a machine that can hold a mind that
	       >thinks 1,000,000 times normal versus one that runs at ordinary  
	       >speed should be considered.  It might be the case
	       >that normal speeds acheived by time-sharing neurons fast would
	       >bring the cost down by a huge factor, say, up to
	       >1,000,000 times.
	       
I was assuming uploading would not be possible before the age of
nanotechnology, after that everything would be either impossible
or dirt cheap. Could uploading happen without nanotechnology?
Perhaps. The computing machinery to receive the upload  MIGHT be
possible if you were willing to pay some huge bills. A more
serious problem is how you would get a readout of the information 
in a brain. Maybe we could use a MRI 
						       
 A run of the mill Magnetic Resonance Imaging machine (MRI) can
make a 3D image of an entire LIVING , conscious brain without
radiation. It can revel structures so small it would take a good
magnifying glass to see. If the resolution were 100 times better
perhaps we would have enough information to upload , especially
if we also had on record the persons genetic code and so knew
the general layout of that particular brain.

 I am not an expert on MRI but even I can think of two ways to
increase resolution, there are probably many others.

  1) Improve the software. Recently Dr. Aaron G Filler of the
University of Washington was able to image tiny nerve fibers for
the first time. He did this by rewriting the software that 
interprets the faint signals given off by a MRI machine and
converts them into pictures.

  2) Increase the magnetic field. The average hospital MRI has a
field of about 3000 gauss although  some cheap models only have
500. The strongest magnetic field ever produced was 372,000
gauss  at the National Magnet Laboratory at MIT. Using a field
this strong in a MRI machine big enough to scan a brain would 
not be a trivial endeavor but is probably easier than nanotechnology. 
As far as I know  no new science  would be needed just excellent engineering.

