Even better than Kevlar, is Spectra, a high strength polyethelyne finbe
(fiber) made by Allied-Signal.  The woven faboric is specifically listed 
for applications like: ballistic vests, riot shields, body shields,
aircraft armor.  To quote from their literature:

"Spectra Shield is a trademark for a patented process which utilizes a
unidirectional resin/fiber matrix, layers of which are bonded at a 0
degree/90 degree orientation.  The resultant products have ballistic
properties currently unmatched by any other fiber system.  Spectra
Shield comes in two product constructions:

* For rigid armor

Uses two plies of unidirectional Spectra prepreg bonded in a 0/90 degree
orientation.  This is recommended for use in rigid composite armor with
multiple layers pressed together depending on the threat level required.

* For flexible armor

Uses two plies of unidirectional Spectra prepreg which are laminated
together with low density polyethylene film facings.  This is used for
flexible body armor.  The film allows the layers to move smoothly upon
each other for maximum flexibility.  Threat levels determine the layers
of Spectra Shield utilized

Properties

* A strain wave velocity of 12,300 m/sec which allows the system to
efficiently disperse the energy of a projectile

* The unidirectional design allows more fibers to engage a projectile
at impact, resulting in excellant multi-hit performance.

* Unique design allows the fibers to remain in constant tension to
protect against a ballistic threat.  Woven systems must overcome their
"crimp" before they are placed in tension"

I am not giving them a product endorsement, but I would want people to
have the best material available for roll-your-own armor.  Their phone
number as of 1991 was (804)520-3321 (Petersburg, VA).  If I was making
ballistic armor for myself, I would rig up a test sample using the
fabric, typical clothing layers, some beef to represent the body, take
it out to a firing range, and shoot at it.

