>that further reduces much of the nutritional content worthless. 

I've certainly read about "easier assimilation of powders", but can't
find the references now.  It may be absorption speed that was referred
to, rather than bioavailability.  Scanning through my life extension
books, I haven't come across any discussion of absorption of powders vs
pills generally, so it appears they don't see it as a significant issue.
If you have any more info on the comparative bioavailability of powders
and tablets/capsules it would be interesting.

Powders would be particularly useful if, say, the first symptoms of a
cold had become apparent, and it was necessary to act quickly to smash it
in its tracks.  Vitamin C is absorbed into the blood through mucous
membranes of the mouth, as well as by the upper part of the small
intestine.  And taking high doses by capsules would mean taking a lot of
them, risking mechanical irritation.  The high dosage would not be
absorbed so efficiently, but excreted vitamins are still useful in
protecting against bowel and bladder cancer.

A number of books mention the problem of allergic reactions to fillers
and binders in vitamin tablets (e.g. headaches, upset stomach, gout,
arthritis, fatigue and depression -- cf. Pearson & Shaw LE-APSA pp 305-6).
These reactions didn't occur with gelatin capsules though, although there
was the possibility of mechanical irritation.  It would be a pity if some
people gave up with supplementation because of side effects, or because
of not wanting to have to swallow pills every day.  If the FDA etc manage
to ban dosages much above the RDA (to maintain work for the Billary
Unhealth Plan / National Unhealth Service etc) it would mean even more
pills to be taken.  Does anyone know if they intend to ban powders?

