Date: Sun, 16 Jan 1994 00:29:34 -0500
From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Children as Property

  Dani Eder writes: 
  
>If you tell me that a child has the 'potential' of becoming intelligent
>enough to contract, well then so does a dog, given sufficient input
>from advanced technology.  The child can't do it itself, it needs
>lots of resources provided by adults... 
 
   Exactly my point - potential rights to potential intelligent beings.
 
However, I have lots of problems here:
 
  - all relevant abilities are too complex to be put in a couple of
    general categories; logically, we should recognize the variety
    of entities with a variety of intermediate statuses between property
    and an independent intelligent beings, with rights gradually shifting
    from the owner to the 'child'. This process may be different for
    different people, so the transfer of rights better be dependent on
    the child's abilities, not on age.
 
  - there is a difference between the ability to 'understand the
    concept of a promise of future action for some present reward 
    or vice versa', i.e. the idea of what a contract is, and
    application of this idea in particular cases - and the ability
    to *deliver* on a contract that would be of any use to anybody.
    A 5yo can promise you - with a clear understanding of what she
    is doing - to be 'a good girl' in exchange for something.
    But she is going to be 'good' while consuming *your* resources...
    She can also promise to buy you the Moon tomorrow, but she can't
    deliver...   However, an adult dog usually understands the idea
    of service for food/protection, *and* can deliver you something
    of real value - and thus shouldn't be considered property.
 
  - there are lots of other cases when [non-children] humans are unable,
    permanently or temporarily, to understand a contract or deliver
    [anything useful] on it.  Old, retarded, handicapped, sick people.
    Should an entity not be considered a property only because something
    that it evolved from once qualified for a higher status?
    If a baby can't be respected for qualities that it will gain in the
    future, then a senile/retarded[/dead?] person should not be judged
    by the qualities s/he had in the past...
    
    What if the person is temporarily unable to contract and/or deliver?
    (if we agree that any inability is fixable given the right technology,
    then the 'temporary' inability is different only in that the technology
    is actually available, and is going to be applied - but that doesn't
    change anything)
    Suppose you get sick/drunk/tired or fall asleep.  Of course, when your
    state changes, you will be able to contract. But so will your computer...
    Of course, you can sign contracts claiming ownership of your temporarily
    sleeping or drunk self - but then you can also own your own corpse?
    For how long?  Should we respect rights of [temporarily] inexistent
    intelligent beings? BTW, who is considered an 'owner' of the cryonic
    patients and who - and how - will be *legally* punished if the bodies
    are damaged or destroyed by the caretaker?
 
Etc.
 
 
   -- Sasha?
 
